## Integrated Dungeon-Crawler Game Systems Architecture

A cohesive foundation of interconnected gameplay systems that together enable a playable single-player RPG experience. The architecture consists of six core pillars: (1) **Dungeon Generation & Layout** provides the navigable world foundation; (2) **Enemy AI & Behavior** introduces challenge and drama through intelligent NPC decision-making (patrol → awareness → tactics); (3) **Combat & Damage System** forms the core interaction loop for player-enemy encounters with state consistency invariants; (4) **Inventory & Loot Management** drives player progression and exploration incentives; (5) **Quest System & Goals** layers narrative motivation and pacing on top of mechanical progression; and (6) **Save/Load & Persistence** enables long-play sessions by retaining character, dungeon, and inventory state across sessions.

These systems are deeply coupled: combat feeds into loot drops; loot feeds quest rewards and progression; enemy AI must model player threat from damage logic; quests target enemies; and all stateful elements must serialize for persistence. The architecture respects SPEC.md §V4 (MeshBasicMaterial, no lights) and requires incremental complexity: begin with stateless mechanics (patrol, simple detection, basic damage), then layer awareness and coordination as systems stabilize.
